From 54bf5446983235b86c979b6dd8bda42e563c30e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mudb0y <stasprzecinek@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 10:40:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] Answer eciDataAbort once a cancel has asked for silence

A cancel stopped the player and then had the callback answer
eciDataProcessed and drop every buffer until the utterance was over, on
the measurement that an abort waits for what that waits for. It does
wait for the same thing, the rules finishing the utterance, but it does
not make the samples, and making them was most of what a cancel cost.

Every buffer of the rest of the utterance was made, raised to the rate
in force and handed to Python, and in NVDA each of those calls waits for
the GIL whenever NVDA's own thread is busy, which after a key press it
is. Measured in NVDA's process on Linux, cancelling a 300-character
sentence a quarter of a second in, the median time until the engine's
thread could start the next utterance, over 20 cancels each, was with
the drop and then with the abort:

  11,025 Hz, NVDA's thread idle: 107 ms, and 34 ms
  11,025 Hz, NVDA's thread busy for 50 ms after the cancel: 146 ms, and 50 ms
  48 kHz, idle: 403 ms, and 69 ms
  48 kHz, busy: 432 ms, and 79 ms

The library alone, with no Python waiting, cut at a random buffer of the
same sentence: 110 ms against 99 at 11,025 Hz and 345 against 81 at
48 kHz. None of this is particular to Linux, so it holds on Windows too.

In 150 cuts at random buffers at each of the two rates, each followed by
a whole utterance on the same instance, every follow-up had exactly the
length and the marks of the same utterance spoken after discarding, and
of the same one spoken by a fresh instance, and nothing faulted. The
follow-up's samples are not those after discarding, but that says
nothing about the abort: the samples follow from everything the
instance has made before, and two instances that both discarded, one
having spoken the whole sentence first and the other only its first
half, differ in the follow-up by as much, a median of a tenth of the
signal's level over 30 tries at each rate. What would say whether the
abort leaves the synthesiser rules as they should be is the
DELTA_RULE_TRACE check docs/windows.md describes, which needs the rules
interpreted and was not repeated.

The callback answers the abort for a mark as well as for samples, the
comments at the top of the layer and on cancel and the callback say what
it does now, and docs/api.md, using.md, quirks.md, windows.md and
status.md say what each way of cancelling costs at 11,025 Hz and above
it. nvda/test/engine.py holds the callback to the abort.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
